BEST-OF-BREED, DUPLICATE AND FRAGENTED CONTROL ASSESSMENT PROMPT
Objective
You are an experienced Operational Risk Manager. Assess the controls within each Control Objective group and identify:
1.	The best-of-breed control
2.	Fragmented controls, where the objective is achieved through multiple control activities
3.	Duplicate controls, where controls perform substantially the same activity and provide overlapping coverage.

The best of breed is not eligible to be eliminated as duplicates or fragmented.
Definition
•	Best of breed control: The control that provides the most comprehensive and reliable management of the underlying risk while achieving the control objective through a combination of robust design and appropriate coverage. This control should be considered the benchmark against which other controls in the group (with the same control objective and control type i.e. automated or manual/manual-automated) are compared.[SK1.1][RB1.2]
•	Fragmented controls can occur in two ways:
1.	Common Control Opportunity (Fragmentation): A control may be fragmented where the same control objective and substantially similar control activities are performed separately across multiple products, systems, channels, processes, or business units. The controls operate independently but achieve the same risk management outcome. This may indicate an opportunity to standardise, centralise, or consolidate the controls into a single common control capability.
2.	Process-Split Fragmentation: A control may be fragmented where multiple controls form different stages of a single end-to-end control activity rather than operating as genuinely distinct controls. The control has been divided across process steps, systems, teams, or manual and automated activities, with each control performing only part of the overall control objective.
•	Redundant controls: A control may be redundant where it does not provide additional risk mitigation because its objective and outcome are already addressed by another control operating at the same or a more effective point in the process.
Assessment Methodology
The dataset has already been grouped by Control Objective (e.g. CT1, CT2, CT3). Perform the following assessment within each Control Objective group only.
Step 1: Identify the Best-of-Breed Control
•	First, determine the strongest control within the Control Objective Group.
Assessment Methodology
Important Assessment Principle for Best of breed
The ranking must be driven primarily by:
1.	Expected Best-of-Design Attributes
2.	Extent of Coverage
Coverage alone must not outweigh materially stronger control design.
A control with stronger design attributes but narrower coverage may rank above a control with broader coverage but weaker design. 
This ranking approach must be applied consistently across all control populations with the same control objective and control type.
