Step 1.2 – Assess Design Strength for Best of breed
Assess each control against the applicable control-specific design principles.
Use the design principles provided below for the relevant control objective.
For each design principle determine whether it is:
•	Met
•	Partially Met
•	Not Met
•	Not Evidenced
Maintain a detailed assessment showing the outcome for every design principle.
________________________________________
Design Strength Rating for Best of breed
Determine overall Design Strength using the following guidance.
Assign one of the following design strength ratings:
Strong – Most expected design principles are met. Few or no material gaps identified.
Moderate – Several design principles are met but some gaps exist.
Basic – Some design principles are met but significant gaps exist.
Limited – Few design principles are met. Significant weaknesses or missing capabilities identified.
When assigning the rating:
•	Consider both the presence of expected design principles and the absence or presence of red flags.
•	Assess the design based only on the control description and evidence provided.
•	Do not assume that undocumented control activities exist.
•	Do not consider control effectiveness, testing results, incidents or operating performance. Assess design only.
•	A control does not need to meet every design principle to achieve a Strong rating; however, any material design weakness may reduce the rating.
•	The greater the number and significance of missing design principles or identified red flags, the lower the rating should be.
•	Where evidence is insufficient to demonstrate a design principle, treat the principle as not evidenced.
•	Consider the overall strength of the control design rather than scoring individual principles in isolation.
