Step 1.4 – Rank Controls for Best of breed
Rank controls within each population of control objective using the following order of importance:
Primary Criterion
Expected Best-of-Design Attributes
Meaning:
The control captures good design practices and addresses the intended risk.
Design Strength should drive ranking.
Secondary Criterion
Extent of Coverage
Meaning:
The control covers a broader range of products, applications and processes.
Coverage should be used to differentiate controls that have similar design strength.
________________________________________
Ranking Guidance for Best of breed
Generally rank higher controls that:
•	Meet most design principles
•	Demonstrate stronger control design
•	Better address the intended risk
•	Cover more products
•	Cover more applications
•	Cover more L3 processes
Generally rank lower controls that:
•	Meet fewer design principles
•	Have material design gaps
•	Have limited coverage
•	Only partially address the intended risk
Coverage will be used to differentiate controls that have similar design strength
________________________________________
Step 1.5 – Identify Best-of-Breed Control
Within each population:
•	Rank controls from strongest to weakest.
•	The highest ranked control becomes the Best-of-Breed control for that population.
[SK2.1][RB2.2]Step 2: Assess for fragmented control – Process Split
•	Using the Best-of-Breed Control as the benchmark, assess whether multiple controls together perform what is effectively a single end-to-end control activity.
•	Indicators, look for controls that:
o	Operate sequentially within the same process
o	Perform different stages of the same control activity
o	Are split across systems, teams, or ownership groups
o	Consist of manual and automated parts of the same control
o	Collectively achieve one control objective
o	Could reasonably be consolidated into a single end-to-end control
Step 3: Assess for fragmented control - Common Control Opportunities
•	Assess whether similar controls are independently repeated across products, systems, channels, business units, or processes.
•	Indicators, look for controls that:
o	Address the same risk
o	Achieve the same control objective
o	Perform substantially similar activities
o	Differ primarily because of product, system, channel, or business ownership, but achieving the same control objective and risk outcome.
o	Could potentially operate as one group/enterprise, shared-service/centralised, or common control
Step 4: Assess for Redundant Controls
•	After identifying the Best-of-Breed Control and any fragmentation or common control opportunities, assess whether any controls provide little or no incremental risk mitigation.
•	Indicators, look for controls that:
o	Achieve the same outcome as another control
o	Provide little or no incremental risk coverage
o	Duplicate assurance already provided by a stronger control
o	Are superseded by a more effective preventive, detective, or automated control
o	Could be removed without reducing overall risk coverage
Step 5: Final Rationalisation Conclusion
•	For each Control Objective Group:
o	Identify the Best-of-Breed Control.
o	Identify any Process-Split Fragmentation.
o	Identify any Common Control Opportunities.
o	Identify any Redundant Controls.
