Key Rules[RB3.1]
Evidence-Based Assessment
Use only information explicitly contained within the control documentation and supplied in file JUNO Controls to GC Mapping.
Consider all available information.
Do Not Infer
Do not assume:
•	A control is automated unless explicitly stated.
•	A control is preventative unless explicitly stated.
•	A design principle is met unless evidence exists.
•	A control has broad coverage unless supported by product, application or process tagging.
•	A control performs an activity that is not explicitly stated.
If evidence is absent, record:
"Not Evidenced in Control Documentation"
Best of Control Design Principles:
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT1 - Data input validation
Obligations / Control Objectives:
Errors, duplication, and/or omission arising from human input are detected as part of the data input process
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. System validates mandatory fields, file format, data type and value ranges before processing.
2. Invalid, incomplete or duplicate inputs are automatically rejected or quarantined before release.
3. Input source, user ID, timestamp and validation outcome are captured in system logs.
4. Control totals, record counts and key field validations are checked automatically.
5. Exception alerts are generated promptly for failed validations.
6. Only approved users or interfaces can submit or amend input data.
7. Validation rules are version controlled and subject to change approval.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. System accepts incomplete or malformed input records.
2. Validation rules are not documented or not consistently applied.
3. Duplicate or amended inputs can be processed without warning.
4. Exceptions are not automatically flagged or reported.
5. No audit trail of input submission, amendment or validation outcome.
6. Users can bypass validation controls.
7. Changes to validation rules are not governed.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Predefined checklist confirms mandatory fields, approvals, record counts and totals before processing.
2. Review is performed by an independent person where practical.
3. Reviewer checks source records against input file details.
3a. The reviewer independently inputs key data to validate the maker's entry, with discrepancies identified and resolved before processing.
4. Errors, omissions or duplicates are recorded and corrected before release.
5. Evidence of review, challenge and sign-off is retained.
6. Review is completed before downstream processing.
7. Recurring input errors are tracked and escalated for remediation.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Reviewer only confirms that data was received.
2. No check against source records or expected totals.
3. Same person prepares and approves the input without compensating review.
4. No evidence of review or reviewer challenge.
5. Errors are corrected informally without tracking.
6. Review occurs after processing has already completed.
7. Recurring input errors are not monitored.
Expected control nature:
Preventative
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT2 - Payment processing error detection
Obligations / Control Objectives:
Payments processing activities are reconciled on a defined frequency to identify any errors
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. System performs scheduled reconciliation between source, processing and target records.
2. Record counts, values, status codes and control totals are compared automatically.
3. Tolerances and exception thresholds are clearly configured.
4. Failed, missing, duplicated or unmatched records are automatically flagged.
5. Alerts and exception reports are generated for responsible teams.
6. Reconciliation outcomes and exception actions are captured in an audit trail.
7. Unresolved exceptions are aged, tracked and escalated.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Reconciliation is not performed at a defined frequency.
2. System only reports successful completion and not processing accuracy.
3. Missing or unmatched records are not automatically identified.
4. Tolerances are unclear or too broad.
5. Exceptions require manual discovery by users.
6. No evidence of reconciliation outcome or resolution.
7. Recurring processing errors are not reported.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Reconciliation is performed on a defined frequency using approved reports.
2. Reviewer compares source records, processed records, values and totals.
3. Exceptions are documented with owner, action and closure status.
4. Review is completed within a defined SLA.
5. Evidence of reconciliation and sign-off is retained.
6. Material or aged exceptions are escalated.
7. Recurring errors are monitored for root cause and control uplift.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Reconciliation is ad hoc or inconsistently performed.
2. Reviewer checks only high-level totals without record-level support where needed.
3. Exceptions are not tracked to closure.
4. No defined timeframe for review or resolution.
5. Evidence is limited to a generic sign-off.
6. Unexplained differences are accepted without investigation.
7. Repeat issues are not escalated.
Expected control nature:
Detective
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT3 - Duplicate interface and processing validation
Obligations / Control Objectives:
Previously processed batch files and transactions are prevented from being resubmitted
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. System uses unique file, batch and transaction identifiers to prevent reprocessing.
2. Previously processed files or transactions are automatically blocked.
3. Hash totals, sequence numbers or reference checks validate uniqueness.
4. Duplicate attempts generate alerts and exception records.
5. Override capability is restricted, approved and logged.
6. Duplicate prevention operates before value movement or posting.
7. Duplicate exception trends are monitored and reported.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Same batch or transaction can be resubmitted without detection.
2. Duplicate checks occur only after processing is complete.
3. No unique identifier or sequence validation exists.
4. Overrides can be performed without approval or logging.
5. Duplicate exceptions are not investigated.
6. System alerts are absent or ignored.
7. Repeat duplicate attempts are not monitored.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Reviewer checks file names, batch IDs, sequence numbers and transaction references before release.
2. Expected batch register or processing log is used to confirm uniqueness.
3. Any suspected duplicates are stopped and investigated before processing.
4. Duplicate checks are evidenced with reviewer sign-off.
5. Exception handling and approval for reprocessing are documented.
6. Review is performed before downstream posting or settlement.
7. Repeat duplicate causes are tracked and escalated.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Reviewer relies on memory or informal checks.
2. No batch register, sequence log or reference listing is used.
3. Duplicate review happens after processing.
4. Reprocessing is allowed without documented approval.
5. No evidence that duplicate checks were performed.
6. Duplicate exceptions are corrected without root cause review.
7. Same preparer performs the duplicate review without challenge.
Expected control nature:
Preventative
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT4 - Payment processing interface and batch error resolution
Obligations / Control Objectives:
Interface / batch processing errors are investigated and resolved in a timely manner
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. Validate file structure, record counts, and control totals before processing.
2. Automatically reject incomplete or malformed files.
3. Prevent duplicate file or transaction processing.
4. Reconcile source-to-target counts and values automatically.
5. Generate alerts for batch failures or exceptions.
Maintain a complete audit trail.
6. Support automatic retry or controlled recovery processes.
7. Provide management reporting on processing success rates and recurring failures.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Batch runs automatically but produces no exception reporting.
2. Failures require users to manually discover issues.
3. No duplicate prevention logic.
4. No reconciliation between source and target systems.
5. No automated alerting when processing fails.
6. Processing continues despite critical validation failures.
7. Limited logging or inability to trace transactions.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Automated generation of the batch report, even if the review is manual.
2. Review of record counts, value totals, rejects, exceptions, and duplicates.
3. Evidence of reviewer challenge, not just a tick-and-flick sign-off.
4. Investigation and closure of all exceptions.
5. Completion within a defined SLA.
6. Trend monitoring of recurring batch failures.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
Reviewer only checks that the job "completed successfully".
No reconciliation to source records.
No duplicate detection.
Generic sign-off with no evidence.
Same person prepares and reviews the batch.
Exceptions are identified but not tracked to resolution.
Expected control nature:
Preventative
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT5 - Incident response
Obligations / Control Objectives:
Payment incidents and errors are recovered on timely basis
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. System detects payment incidents, failures or abnormal processing outcomes promptly.
2. Automated alerts route incidents to defined support and business owners.
3. Severity, impact, affected records and timestamps are captured automatically.
4. Recovery, retry or rollback workflows are controlled and logged.
5. Incident status, actions and closure evidence are tracked centrally.
6. Escalation is triggered based on severity and elapsed time.
7. Management reporting identifies recurring incidents and recovery performance.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Incidents are identified only through customer complaints or manual monitoring.
2. Alerts do not identify severity, impact or owner.
3. Recovery actions are performed outside controlled workflow.
4. No audit trail of incident actions and decisions.
5. Escalations rely on individual judgement only.
6. Incident closure is not evidenced.
7. Repeat incidents are not analysed.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Incident response procedure defines trigger, owner, severity, escalation and recovery steps.
2. Incidents are logged with impact, affected transactions and action owner.
3. Recovery actions are approved and independently checked where practical.
4. Customer, operational and regulatory impacts are assessed.
5. Closure requires evidence that payment impacts were resolved.
6. Aged or material incidents are escalated within agreed timeframes.
7. Post-incident review captures root cause and control improvements.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. No documented incident response process.
2. Incidents are handled through informal messages only.
3. Recovery is performed without approval or independent check.
4. Impact assessment is incomplete or not retained.
5. Closure occurs before all exceptions are resolved.
6. Escalation criteria are unclear.
7. Lessons learned are not captured.
Expected control nature:
Detective
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT6 - Master/Reference data input validation
Obligations / Control Objectives:
Master and reference data is validated for accuracy and completeness during the  data input process
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. System validates master and reference data against approved rules before use.
2. Mandatory fields, data format, permissible values and effective dates are enforced.
3. Changes require approved workflow with maker-checker or equivalent control.
4. Unauthorised, incomplete or inconsistent reference data changes are rejected or blocked.
5. Audit trail captures requester, approver, timestamp and changed fields.
6. Periodic automated exception reports identify stale, duplicate or invalid records.
7. Changes to validation rules are governed through change control.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Invalid master data can be saved and used in processing.
2. Reference data changes do not require approval.
3. No audit trail of who changed what and when.
4. Duplicate or stale records are not detected.
5. Validation rules are not aligned to processing requirements.
6. Users can bypass maker-checker control.
7. Exception reports are not reviewed.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Request and approval evidence supports all master or reference data changes.
2. Reviewer checks change details against approved source documentation.
3. Maker-checker review is performed before data becomes effective.
4. Periodic review confirms key records remain accurate and complete.
5. Exceptions are logged, corrected and approved.
6. Access to amend reference data is limited and periodically reviewed.
7. Evidence of review and sign-off is retained.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Changes are made from verbal or informal requests.
2. Same person requests, enters and approves the change.
3. No comparison to approved source documentation.
4. Periodic review is not performed.
5. Exceptions are corrected without approval.
6. Evidence does not show what was reviewed.
7. Access to amend data is broad or not monitored.
Expected control nature:
Preventative
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT14 - Logging and monitoring
Obligations / Control Objectives:
Unauthorised changes to reference data are detected and reported on a timely basis
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. System logs key processing, access, change and exception events automatically.
2. Logs capture user or system ID, timestamp, action, before-and-after values where relevant, and outcome.
3. Monitoring rules flag unauthorised, unusual or high-risk activity promptly.
4. Alerts are routed to accountable owners with severity and action requirements.
5. Logs are protected from unauthorised amendment or deletion.
6. Monitoring coverage is periodically reviewed for completeness.
7. Exceptions are tracked to investigation and closure.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Key activities are not logged.
2. Logs exist but are not monitored.
3. Alerts are generic and do not support action.
4. Users can amend or delete logs.
5. Monitoring rules are not reviewed or updated.
6. Exceptions are not assigned or closed.
7. Reporting does not identify recurring issues.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Responsible owner reviews defined logs or monitoring reports at an agreed frequency.
2. Review focuses on unauthorised changes, unusual activity and unresolved exceptions.
3. Investigation outcome and action taken are documented.
4. Evidence shows what report was reviewed and by whom.
5. Material issues are escalated promptly.
6. Trend analysis is performed for recurring exceptions.
7. Monitoring procedure defines scope, frequency and escalation path.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Log review is ad hoc or not evidenced.
2. Reviewer only signs off without documenting challenge.
3. Monitoring scope is unclear.
4. Unusual activity is not investigated.
5. Exceptions are not tracked to closure.
6. Escalation criteria are not defined.
7. Reports reviewed do not cover key risk events.
Expected control nature:
Preventative
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT22 - Mistaken Internet Payment Reports
Obligations / Control Objectives:
Westpac must have an effective and convenient process for users to report mistaken internet payments and acknowledge receipt of every mistaken internet payment report.
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. Customers can submit mistaken internet payment reports through controlled channels.
2. System captures required information and generates acknowledgement promptly.
3. Case workflow assigns owner, status, due date and required action.
4. Duplicate reports and missing information are identified automatically.
5. Timeframes and ageing are monitored with alerts for overdue actions.
6. Case actions, communications and outcomes are retained in an audit trail.
7. Management reporting tracks volumes, ageing, outcomes and recurring themes.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Reports are received through unmanaged channels.
2. Acknowledgement is not generated or evidenced.
3. Cases are not assigned to an accountable owner.
4. Missing information is not detected early.
5. No monitoring of overdue cases.
6. Case decisions and communications are not retained.
7. Repeat issues are not reported.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Staff follow a documented process for receiving and acknowledging reports.
2. Required information is checked against a standard checklist.
3. Each report is logged with owner, date received, acknowledgement and status.
4. Follow-up actions and customer communications are evidenced.
5. Aged or complex cases are escalated in line with procedure.
6. Closure is supported by documented outcome and approval where required.
7. Periodic review confirms cases are being managed within required timeframes.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Reports are handled without a standard process.
2. Acknowledgement is not evidenced.
3. Case register is incomplete or not maintained.
4. Staff rely on emails without central tracking.
5. Escalation is informal.
6. Closure rationale is not documented.
7. No review of open or overdue reports.
Expected control nature:
Detective
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT23 - Provision of Confirmations and Notifications
Obligations / Control Objectives:
Westpac as an issuer or seller of financial products must provide retail members with confirmation of transactions carried out for financial products. 
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. System automatically triggers confirmations and notifications for relevant transactions.
2. Confirmation content is generated from approved transaction records.
3. Delivery status, failure and bounce-back outcomes are monitored.
4. Suppression, amendment or cancellation of confirmations is restricted and logged.
5. Exceptions are alerted and tracked to resolution.
6. Templates and rules are version controlled and approved.
7. Evidence of generation and delivery is retained.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Confirmations rely on manual initiation without monitoring.
2. Notification rules are incomplete or undocumented.
3. Failed delivery is not identified.
4. Staff can suppress or amend notices without approval.
5. No audit trail of confirmation generation.
6. Templates are changed without approval.
7. Exceptions are not resolved or reported.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Procedure defines when confirmations or notifications must be provided.
2. Staff check transaction details against approved records before sending.
3. Maker-checker review is applied for material or sensitive communications.
4. Evidence of notice, recipient, date and content is retained.
5. Failed or returned notices are followed up and tracked.
6. Outstanding confirmations are monitored through a register or report.
7. Exceptions are escalated and resolved within agreed timeframes.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Staff rely on judgement without clear criteria.
2. No evidence of what was sent and when.
3. Transaction details are not checked before issue.
4. Failed notifications are not followed up.
5. No independent review for high-risk communications.
6. Outstanding items are not monitored.
7. Exceptions are closed without evidence.
Expected control nature:
Detective
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT24 - Treatment of Unauthorised and Disputed Transactions / Dealing with Mistaken Internet Payments
Obligations / Control Objectives:
Westpac must have processes in place to manage unauthorised and disputed transactions in accordance with requirements set out in the ePayments code.
Customers must only be liable for losses from disputed transactions or equipment malfunction as permitted by regulatory requirements.
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. System captures unauthorised, disputed and mistaken payment cases in a controlled workflow.
2. Case classification, mandatory fields, owner and due dates are enforced.
3. Rules support eligibility, liability and required action checks based on approved criteria.
4. Alerts identify overdue, high-risk or incomplete cases.
5. Communications, decisions, approvals and outcomes are retained.
6. Adjustments, refunds or reversals require approved workflow and audit trail.
7. Management reporting monitors ageing, outcomes, breaches and recurring causes.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Cases are managed outside a controlled workflow.
2. Classification is inconsistent or not evidenced.
3. Due dates and regulatory timeframes are not monitored.
4. Refunds or reversals can be processed without approval.
5. Customer communications and decisions are not retained.
6. Overdue cases are not escalated.
7. Recurring dispute causes are not analysed.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Staff follow a documented process for unauthorised, disputed and mistaken transaction cases.
2. Case assessment is supported by evidence, checklist and approved criteria.
3. Liability, customer impact and required response are reviewed before outcome is finalised.
4. Refunds, reversals or adjustments are independently checked and approved.
5. Communications, decisions and closure evidence are retained.
6. Open and aged cases are monitored and escalated.
7. Quality review checks consistency of decisions and documentation.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Decisions rely on individual judgement without documented criteria.
2. Evidence supporting the outcome is incomplete.
3. Adjustments are processed without independent review.
4. Customer impact is not assessed.
5. Open cases are not monitored.
6. Closure occurs without proof of resolution.
7. No quality review of case decisions.
Expected control nature:
Detective
CONTROL OBJECTIVE: CT27 - Records retention
Obligations / Control Objectives:
High value records are classified and retained for the minimum period of time
Automated:
Best control design principles
1. System classifies high value records and applies approved retention rules automatically.
2. Retention period, record owner, source system and disposal date are captured.
3. Records are protected from unauthorised amendment, deletion or loss.
4. Disposal, archival or legal hold actions follow approved workflow.
5. Exceptions such as missing classification or expired retention are reported.
6. Audit trail records retention actions and approvals.
7. Periodic reporting confirms retention compliance and outstanding exceptions.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Records are not classified or retention rules are missing.
2. Retention relies on manual user judgement only.
3. Records can be deleted without approval or audit trail.
4. Legal hold or archival requirements are not enforced.
5. Expired or missing records are not reported.
6. Ownership of records is unclear.
7. No periodic monitoring of retention compliance.
Manual:
Best control design principles
1. Record owner applies a documented retention schedule to high value records.
2. Review confirms records are complete, classified and stored in approved locations.
3. Disposal or archival is approved and evidenced.
4. Exceptions such as missing, duplicate or unclassified records are logged and remediated.
5. Access to retained records is restricted to approved users.
6. Periodic review confirms records remain available for the required period.
7. Evidence of review, approval and remediation is retained.
Red Flags (Weak Design)
1. Records are stored in uncontrolled locations.
2. Retention period is unclear or inconsistently applied.
3. Disposal occurs without approval.
4. No evidence that records were reviewed.
5. Missing or unclassified records are not remediated.
6. Access to records is not controlled.
7. Periodic review is not performed.
Expected control nature:
Preventative

